WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.620
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gold standard;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.100
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.800
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.900
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
I do!

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.080
Don’t tell me you haven’t
put some serious thought

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.120
on how to avoid dementia:

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.720
a brain disease that has
no real cure as of yet.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.169
Here’s our first story.

00:00:33.561 --> 00:00:35.940
Although it's known that
plant-based foods are important

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.280
for physical health,
less is known about

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.340
the relationship between plant-
based foods and cognitive health.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.960
Now in terms of preventing Alzheimer's
and other forms of actual dementia,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.020
there's data that those who consume
meat, including poultry and fish,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.780
have 2 to 3 times the risk
of developing dementia

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.770
compared with vegetarians,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.800
but what about just like
day-to-day function?

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.160
Greater adherence to a more
plant-based dietary pattern

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.000
was related to better
performance on all cognitive

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.860
tasks researchers measured.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.220
One possible mechanism that
could have been thought

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.320
to underlie the results
is body weight. I mean,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.560
plant-based diets reduce BMI,
and lower BMI has been associated

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.280
with better cognitive function,
but they still found a connection

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.440
between more plants
and better brain function

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.360
even after controlling for weight.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.460
Another possible mechanism linking
diet and cognition is inflammation.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.180
That's how saturated fat impairs
the memory of lab rats,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.120
through brain inflammation.
And since fiber can be

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.640
anti-inflammatory, and
meat pro-inflammatory,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.520
that may help explain some
effects of plant-based diets

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.720
on health and cognition.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.640
The saturated fat connection appears
to extend to human cognition.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.480
A systematic review and
meta-analysis covering nine studies

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.640
found that increased
saturated fat intake,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.860
which is found mostly
in meat, dairy, and junk,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.700
was associated with a 40% increased
risk of cognitive impairment

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.820
and nearly 90% higher risk
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.180
So wait, does that mean if you
put people on a low-carb diet,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.960
it impairs their brain function?
Yes, it does.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.140
A high-fat diet not only impairs the
heart but also cognitive function.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.520
Men were randomized to just
to 5 days of a high-fat,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.640
low-carbohydrate diet,
or a lower fat diet,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.520
and on the low-carb diet
cognitive tests showed

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.260
they suffered impaired attention,
speed, and mood.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.940
Again, just within days.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.260
Conclusion: raising the level of fat
in your blood not only decreased

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.340
energy production in the heart,
but reduced cognition, which

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.120
suggests that a high-fat diet is
detrimental to the heart and brain.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.240
Now they were thinking
the impaired energy production

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.700
may have accounted for
the brain dysfunction as well,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.800
but oral glycotoxins may
also link high-fat eating with

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.260
a loss of cognitive capacity.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.640
Glycotoxins, also known as advanced
glycation end products or AGEs,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.460
are a class of oxidant
stress-promoting agents,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.080
free radical-promoting agents
implicated in diabetes and aging,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.200
including brain injury due to
Alzheimer's disease and stroke.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.560
The development of Alzheimer's
disease in the first place

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.260
is thought to involve the
accumulation of these AGEs,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.440
which encourage the formation
and deposition of the two

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.820
hallmarks of Alzheimer's:

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.540
neurofibrillary tangles and
amyloid plaques in the brain,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.160
discovered on autopsy.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.280
But it's not just full-blown
dementia. Evidence suggests

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.120
AGEs contribute to
cognitive decline in general.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.500
Dietary advanced glycation
end products are associated

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.040
with a decline in memory.
And since modifying the levels

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.560
of AGEs in the diet
may be relatively easy,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.860
these preliminary results
suggest a simple strategy

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.200
to diminish cognitive compromise.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.400
What are the major sources of
dietary AGEs to stay away from?

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.800
Meat, cooked using high,
dry heat, such as in broiling,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.720
grilling, frying, and roasting.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.200
From my video, "The Best Diet
for Healthy Aging,"

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:09.000
here is the list of all the most
AGE-contaminated foods.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.560
AGEs are not only associated with
getting Alzheimer's in the first place,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.320
but also the progression
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.140
as well as lower cognitive
performance in general,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:24.180
as tracked, interestingly enough,
via skin autofluorescence.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.020
AGE's have a natural fluorescence
that you can pick up

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.580
using a special detector, enabling
a simple non-invasive assessment

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.940
of advanced glycation end product
accumulation in the body.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.200
The more meat you eat, the more
of the AGE skin autofluorescence

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.740
you get, which then correlates
with cognitive impairment.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.500
In fact one of these days
these fluorescence scanners

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.260
may be included in
routine medical check-ups.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:53.820
Since meat is the main high
AGE-food, it should be no surprise

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.760
that AGE skin autofluorescence
measurements are

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.000
significantly lower in those
eating more plant based.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.260
So the data suggest reduction
of food-derived AGEs is feasible

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.780
and may provide effective
treatment strategies

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.455
for our epidemics of Alzheimer's
and metabolic disease.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.520
In our next story, we look at
the best source of lutein,

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.000
the primary carotenoid
antioxidant in the brain.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.020
There's an extensive scientific
literature describing the positive

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.100
impact of dietary plant compounds
on overall health and longevity.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.880
However, it's only now becoming
clear that the consumption of diets

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.320
rich in plant foods can influence neuro-
inflammation, brain inflammation,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.860
leading to the expression of
cytoprotective, cell protective,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.180
and restorative proteins.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.720
Just over the last decade, remarkable
progress has been made

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.260
to realize that oxidative stress and
chronic, low-grade inflammation,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.260
are major risk factors
underlying brain aging,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:54.060
so no wonder antioxidant and
anti-inflammatory foods may help.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.040
The brain is especially vulnerable to
free radical attack, oxidative stress,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.600
due to its high fat content and its
cauldron of high metabolic activity.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.840
You don't want your
brains to go rancid.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.020
So you'd think one of the major
fat-soluble dietary antioxidants

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.400
like beta-carotene would step in, but
the major carotenoid concentrated

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.860
in the brain is actually lutein; the
brain just preferentially sucks it up.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.960
For example, if you
look at the oldest old,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.300
like in the Georgia
centenarian study,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.280
recognizing that oxidation is involved
in age-related cognitive decline,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.420
they figured dietary antioxidants may
play a role in its prevention or delay,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.160
so they looked at eight different ones,
vitamin A, vitamin E, on down the list,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.240
and only lutein was significantly
related to better cognition.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.440
Now in this study they looked
at brain tissue on autopsy.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.880
By then it's a little too late.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.760
So how could you study
the effects of diet

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.620
on the brain while
you're still alive?

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.400
If only there was a way we
could physically look into

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.040
the living brain with
our own two eyes.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.540
There is!
With our own two eyes.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.460
The retina, the back of our eyeball,
is actually an extension

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.340
of our central nervous system,
an outpouching of the brain

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.000
during development, and right
in the middle there's a spot.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.820
This is what the doctor sees
when they look into your eye

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.180
with that bright light.
That spot, called the macula,

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.080
is our HD camera, where you get
the highest resolution vision,

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.820
and it's packed with lutein.
And indeed, levels in the retina

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.700
correspond to levels
in the rest of your brain,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.120
so your eyes can be a
window into your brain.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.300
So now we can finally do
studies on live people

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:42.180
to see if diet can affect
lutein levels in the eyes,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.220
which reflects lutein
levels in the brain,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:47.340
and see if that correlates with
improvements in cognitive function.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.720
And indeed, significant correlations
exist between the amount

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.540
of macular pigment, these
plant pigments like lutein

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.760
in your eye, and
cognitive test scores.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.480
You can demonstrate this
on functional MRI scans,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.500
suggesting lutein and a related
plant pigment called zeaxanthin

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.120
promote cognitive functioning in old
age by enhancing neural efficiency,

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.520
the efficiency by which
our nerves communicate.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.940
Like check out this cool study
on white matter integrity

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.700
using something called
diffusion tensor imaging,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:22.140
which provides unique insights
into brain network connectivity,

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.200
allowing you to follow the nerve
tracts throughout the brain.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.280
And researchers were able to
show enhanced circuit integrity

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.360
based on how much lutein and zeaxanthin
they could see in people's eyes—

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.040
further evidence of a meaningful
relationship between diet and integrity

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:42.060
of our brains, particularly in regions
vulnerable to age-related decline.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.160
So do Alzheimer's patients have
less of this macular pigment?

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.780
Significantly less lutein in their eyes,
significantly less lutein in their blood,

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.660
and a higher occurrence
of macular degeneration,

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.460
where this pigment layer
gets destroyed.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.900
The thickness of this plant pigment
layer in your eyes can be measured

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.280
and may be a potential marker
for the beginnings of Alzheimer's.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.080
Let's not wait that long, though.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.960
We know macular pigment density
is related to cognitive function

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.960
in older people.
What about during middle age?

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:18.000
One apparent consequence of aging
appears to be loss of some aspects

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.260
of cognitive control, which starts
out early, in mid-adulthood,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:27.060
but not in everybody, suggesting
maybe something like diet could

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.180
be driving some of the differences.
Here's a measure of cognitive control,

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.620
showing younger, on average,
do better than older adults.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.400
But older adults who have
high macular pigment,

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.820
lots of lutein in the back of their
eyes, do significantly better.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.300
These results suggest that the
protective role of carotenoids

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:46.620
like lutein within the brain

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.320
may be evident during
early and middle adulthood,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.480
decades prior to the onset of more
apparent cognitive decline later in life.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:00.060
You can take 20-year-olds and
show superior auditory function

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.180
in those with more macular
pigment in their eyes.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.380
Look, the auditory system,
our hearing, like the rest

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:10.260
of the central nervous system,
is ultimately constructed

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.860
and maintained by diet, and
it is therefore, not surprisingly,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.440
sensitive to dietary intake
throughout life,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.320
all the way back to childhood.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.400
Higher macular pigment is associated
with higher academic achievement

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:24.300
among school children.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.320
You can look into a kid's eyes
and get some sense of how well

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.780
they may do in subjects
like math and writing.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.880
This finding is important because
macular lutein is modifiable

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.200
and can be manipulated
by dietary intake.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:45.120
OK, OK, so where is lutein found?

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.720
The avocado and egg
industries like to boast

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.480
about how much of these macular
pigments they have in their products,

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:56.040
but the real superstars
are dark green leafy vegetables.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:01.200
A half cup of kale has 50
times more than an egg,

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.860
a spinach salad,
or a 50-egg omelet?

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.080
And the earlier the better.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.080
Pregnant and breastfeeding women
should definitely be checking off

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.880
my Daily Dozen greens servings.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.240
But it's also apparently
never too late.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.480
While some age-related cognitive
decline is to be expected,

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.622
these effects may be less pronounced
among those eating more green and leafy.

00:11:24.493 --> 00:11:28.908
Finally today, avocados, greens,
and lutein and zeaxanthin supplements

00:11:28.908 --> 00:11:33.600
are put to the test for
improving cognitive function.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.520
Dark green leafy vegetables are packed
with a brain antioxidant called lutein.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.540
And so increasing our greens intake could
be an important public health strategy

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.260
for reducing the risk of visual
or cognitive impairment.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.900
Lutein is the dominant
dietary pigment

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.720
in the retina of the eye
as well as the brain.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.060
And so not surprising
that macular pigment—

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.420
the concentration of lutein in the
center of your eye called the macula—

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.360
was found to be significantly
correlated with levels in the brain,

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:05.100
which may explain the link between
how much of these greens nutrients

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.680
you can see in the back of
the eye and cognitive function.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.460
The neuroprotection is assumed
to be because lutein is

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:16.560
such a powerful antioxidant, but it
also has anti-inflammatory properties.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.200
This relationship between lutein,

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.360
and another greens
nutrient called zeaxanthin,

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.780
and visual and cognitive health
throughout the lifespan is compelling.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:28.800
But that was based on observational
studies, where you observe

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.620
that higher lutein levels and brain
function seem to go together,

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.540
but you don't know if it's cause
and effect until you put it to the test.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.960
Could lutein and zeaxanthin
be supplemented

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.500
as part of a lifestyle intervention
to both improve brain function

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.560
and reduce the probability
of slipping into dementia?

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.920
The reason everyone is so
excited about the possibility

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.000
is because of the hopeful data
from eye health studies

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.480
that have convinced many
ophthalmologists to start recommending

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.780
people start increasing their lutein
and zeaxanthin intake to prevent

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:05.640
and treat macular degeneration, a
leading cause of age-related vision loss.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.680
You don't have to take pills, though.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.400
Adding as little as 60 grams
of spinach a day for a month—

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.700
that's like one-fifth of a 10-ounce
package of frozen spinach—

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.060
can significantly boost macular
pigment in most people.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:21.780
And it not just good for
treating diseased eyes.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.560
A randomized, placebo-controlled study
found that these greens goodies

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.380
can improve visual processing
speed in young healthy people.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.920
That's like when you're trying to
hit a fastball and your body

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.800
has to start reacting before you
even consciously register it—

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.040
with real-world benefits
outside the major leagues,

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.220
improving, for example,
visual driving performance.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.100
OK, but what about cognition?

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.600
A randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial of adults,

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:52.620
average age 73, given the equivalent
of about a half cup a day's worth

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:56.040
of cooked kale, or a full
cup of cooked spinach,

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.460
and got significant improvements
in cognitive function

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:01.920
compared to those randomized
to the placebo.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:06.420
It may even work in young
adults too, average age 21.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:09.600
Daily supplementation with that same
amount of lutein and zeaxanthin

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.380
not only increased their macular pigment,
but resulted in significant improvements

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:17.400
in brain function—spatial memory,
reasoning ability, and complex attention.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.720
Have they ever tried putting
whole foods to the test?

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.720
Hard to get Americans
to eat greens every day,

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:28.080
but not so hard to get them
to eat guacamole.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.860
This study tested the effects of the
intake of avocado on cognition,

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.660
a six-month, randomized,
controlled trial.

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.460
What was the control?

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:40.380
One avocado a day, or a potato
or a cup of chickpeas,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.480
and those in the avocado group

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.780
had a significant improvement
in cognitive function.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:51.900
But to the Avocado Board's chagrin,
so did the tater and chickpea group.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.140
That's the problem with
having healthy placebos.

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.660
Maybe they should have used
iceberg lettuce or something.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:02.340
What about the impact on cognition
of those who really need it,

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:07.260
Alzheimer's disease patients?
Their vision got better, that's good,

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:10.620
but no significant changes
in cognitive function.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.220
Now it's possible that eating whole
foods, like dark green leafies,

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.760
might have worked better than
just the pigments in pill form.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:22.440
Yes, oxidation and inflammation
appear to be key to both Alzheimer's

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.460
and macular degeneration, but neither
disease seems particularly amenable

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:31.740
to late-stage treatments.
That's why prevention is the key.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:35.160
Reducing oxidation and
inflammation in the earliest stages

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:38.546
may be our most promising approach.

